---
import ProsePage from '../../templates/ProsePage.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../config/site.ts';
---

<ProsePage
  locale="en"
  routeKey="privacy"
  title="Privacy"
  description="This site sets no cookies and loads no third-party scripts. Beyond anonymous speed measurement, it collects only what an event registration or newsletter opt-in strictly needs."
>
  <p>
    Short answer: browsing this site collects nothing about you personally. Registering for an event
    or opting into the newsletter collects exactly what those services need, and nothing else.
    Sponsors never receive attendee data — not in aggregate reports either, which only ever contain
    counts.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    None. Not for analytics, not for preferences, not for advertising. Your theme preference (light
    or dark) is stored in <code>localStorage</code> in your own browser and is never transmitted.
  </p>

  <h2>Statistics</h2>
  <p>
    We measure how many people visit which pages through an aggregate counter that cannot
    distinguish individual visitors: no cookies, no fingerprinting, no profiles. We also measure,
    anonymously and without cookies, how fast pages load (the Web Vitals) — not who you are, only
    how fast it was. That speed measurement goes to our own, self-hosted telemetry server, not a
    third-party service. That is also why no consent banner is needed.
  </p>

  <h2>Third parties</h2>
  <p>
    This site loads no fonts, scripts or images from external domains. Links to other sites (company
    websites, meetup pages) do of course take you to those parties, where their own privacy policy
    applies.
  </p>

  <h2>Event registration</h2>
  <p>
    Event registration runs on Meetup. You register for the evening there, and twente.dev stays the
    page that describes the edition. Meetup Inc. is a US service and processes your registration
    data. We ask only what is needed for access, food and safety: your name, email address, dietary
    needs and access needs, plus an optional question about what you are building or looking for.
    This data lives in one registration list, is used for that event and its follow-up, then deleted
    within three months of the event. Cancelling is one reply. Registering for an event never adds
    you to the newsletter.
  </p>

  <h2>Newsletter</h2>
  <p>
    The newsletter is double opt-in: you sign up, and confirm from your own inbox. We record when
    and where you consented. Every edition carries a one-click unsubscribe, open tracking is
    disabled, and the list is never shared or enriched. Unsubscribing removes you immediately.
  </p>

  <h2>If we contact you</h2>
  <p>
    Sometimes we approach an organisation ourselves: a community organiser, a school, a
    municipality, an employer or a venue. When we do, we use contact details that the organisation
    has published itself for being contacted — a contact page, an <code>info@</code> address, a board
    page. We do not guess addresses, buy lists, or use enrichment services.
  </p>
  <p>
    What we record: the organisation, the contact's name and role, the public contact address,
    <strong>the source we took it from</strong>, when we made contact and what came of it. Nothing
    else. The legal basis is legitimate interest (article 6(1)(f) GDPR): building a regional
    technology community means talking to the organisations already doing the work. Our first email
    always tells you where we got your details.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>You can object at any time and we stop immediately</strong> — no balancing, no explanation
    needed. Reply "stop" or email
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. After that we keep only your email
    address and the date you objected, precisely so we do not contact you again by accident. If you
    simply do not reply, we delete your details twelve months after the last contact. Being
    contacted never adds you to the newsletter — you sign up for that yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Photos at events</h2>
  <p>
    Event photography is consent-based: at check-in you can visibly indicate you do not want to be
    photographed, and there is a simple removal route afterwards — no reason required. See the code
    of conduct for how this works in the room.
  </p>

  <h2>Processors</h2>
  <p>
    The site is served by Cloudflare, which also routes mail sent to our addresses. Event
    registration runs on Meetup Inc., a US service, which processes your name, email address and
    your answers to the registration questions. Every mail we send automatically — the newsletter
    first, later also confirmations around an event — goes through Brevo, a French service that
    processes data only on our instructions, inside the EU/GDPR framework. That mail comes from <code
      >send.twente.dev</code
    >; mail a human here writes themselves stays outside it. We name every processor here, and
    update this list when one changes. No data is sold or shared.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask what we hold about you, have it corrected or deleted, and withdraw any consent —
    email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. You will get an answer from a
    human within a week.
  </p>

  <h2>What you submit</h2>
  <p>
    If you submit an event or company, that information becomes public in a git repository,
    including its history. So do not put anything in it that should not be public.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    twente.dev is run by Ryan Grippeling through Webgrip, which is the controller for the data
    described on this page.
  </p>
  <p>
    Webgrip · Koekoekstraat 8, 7523 WZ Enschede, Netherlands ·
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>
</ProsePage>
